00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.900
Hello, everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.300
Today we'll be talking about languages without verb tenses.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.600
Languages without verb tenses? How is that possible?

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:18.479
Speakers of English and other indo-european languages,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.350
and I guess other languages too, might take it for granted that the basic features of their language must be present in other languages as

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.310
well
But that's not always the case

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:33.750
One example of this is verb tense using a particular form of a verb to show the time period of an action past

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:40.409
Present or future as they exist on a timeline in English we basically have these three tenses present tense is

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:46.500
Represented by the simple verb stem like play for example with regular verbs the past tense is

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.969
represented by the stem plus the inflection edie like played and

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:58.590
The simple future tense is formed by placing the modal auxiliary verb will before the infinitive which is based on the verb stem

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:05.999
- - for example we'll play some linguists might argue that this future form is not actually a verb tense

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.229
We'll come back to that later

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:11.249
But some languages don't have verb tenses meaning they don't have different verb

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:19.470
Conjugations to show periods of time this is true of many Creole languages for example of Indonesian and Malaysian of Chinese of many Native American

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:26.189
Languages and a lot more. Here's an example from Indonesian. Here's the verb meaning pay in the past present and future

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.689
Mumbai odda the form of the verb is exactly the same in all three tenses

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:36.779
And this might sound strange to you how can they clearly communicate without being able to say when an action takes place?

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.729
Well first of all it's normally not that hard to guess from context. Just to illustrate

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.439
Let's take the English word put which is the same in the present tense and past tense I?

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.130
Put my keys on the table

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:54.960
So as that sentence of present tense or past tense in the middle of a story taking place in the past it would probably be

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:01.710
Past tense, but if someone were describing that action as part of their daily after work routine, then it would probably be present tense

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:08.879
Context can clarify the time of an action aside from general context. There are other ways of indicating when an action takes place

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:15.329
First there are words and expressions that indicate a specific time that the action takes place like today yesterday

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:21.600
Next week last year etc second there are adverbs that indicate aspect for example

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.380
Sudha Mumbai er which basically means already paid

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:33.929
Sedan Mumbai er which means in the process of paying masse Mumbai er which means still paying these adverbs show aspect

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:41.609
What's the difference between tense and aspect as I mentioned before temps indicates the time period of an action past present or future?

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:47.999
Aspect deals with the way actions unfold in relation to the time line rather than where they occur on the time line

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:55.320
Sudha shows that an action is completed with a focus on the result of the action. We could call this perfect aspect

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.870
sadang marks the imperfective aspect

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:05.790
Showing that an action is in progress, but the action is not necessarily in the present tense Massey. Also marks the imperfective aspect

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:11.040
Showing that an action is continuing but with an emphasis on the fact that it's not yet finished

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:17.339
These are just three of the many aspect markers in Indonesian another language without tenses is Mandarin Chinese

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:24.029
I mentioned this in my video comparing Chinese and Japanese, but unfortunately I messed up my explanation a little bit

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.239
Here's the present tense sentence. I gave in that video watch

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:34.559
Yourself this means I go to school the verb Chi is the same no matter what period of time it takes place in

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:39.209
But there are words that can be placed before or after the verb which show aspect

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:43.679
What should I should I sell this means? I went to school la

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.860
Indicates the perfective aspect viewing an action in its entirety including its completion at a point in time

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:56.279
This is often thought of as a past tense marker, but in certain contexts it can be used with future events for example

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:03.149
Warming can cheela funky need a deal. Wha this means I'll call you after I've had a meal tomorrow

00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:07.199
time can also be indicated with time related vocabulary

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:13.709
So again, what cheerless you so this means yesterday? I went to school. You can see that

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:16.439
We've added the word for yesterday in this sentence

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.830
We actually still need the perfective aspect marker because the verb here is an action verb with a definite start and finish

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:30.660
But in some sentences the perfective aspect marker is not needed for example, so what you see you see this means yesterday

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.969
I rested here. There's no aspect marker and the only indication of time is the word meaning yesterday

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:42.839
Here's a sentence with a different aspect marker. What's that? She she saw the Lu Xiang this means?

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.829
I'm on the way to school. This word sad shows imperfective aspect

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:54.239
Indicating an action that is in progress and again not necessarily in the present and this part at the end here means on the road

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.049
These are two common aspect markers in Mandarin, but there are others

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.049
The main thing to notice with the Indonesian and Mandarin examples is that the verb itself is not conjugated

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:11.609
You simply place a different word an aspect marker next to the verb it's actually very common for languages to not have tense

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.289
But I've never heard of a language that doesn't have aspect in many languages tense and aspect

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.839
combined together to form various verb

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:26.519
conjugations and many people really don't distinguish between the two concepts for example in English we have the present tense as in I work and

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:31.649
We have another form I'm working which is in the present tense, but with progressive aspect

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.709
And we have I have worked which is in the present tense

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.430
but with perfect aspect

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:42.900
Showing a connection between a past event and the present people tend to think of all of these different verb constructions as tenses

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:47.729
But strictly speaking there is more than just tense there there are certain languages that do have tenses

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:53.968
But not all of them in particular. It's quite common for languages to show no distinction between present tense and future tense

00:05:53.969 --> 00:05:55.930
This is the case with Japanese for example

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.739
Which has two tenses past and non past

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:04.169
so cool today must could mean I eat Japanese food in the general sense or

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.680
I'll eat Japanese food in a specific case in the future

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.799
It's normally not hard to tell the difference based on context

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.889
And there's another form day you do or Tomas which represents a continuing action or continuing state

00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:20.519
Incorporating uses of both progressive as well as perfect aspect

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:26.099
People often think this is strictly a present tense form but it can also be used for future actions for example

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.219
Nice your name. I got a lot well kinda

00:06:29.219 --> 00:06:31.799
They say go to state or not they change it on a night

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:37.799
This sentence means I can't believe that around this time next week. I'll already be living in Canada here

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.370
You can see that the verb say katsu stay to is in the toyota form

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:48.989
Representing progressive aspect, but the action takes place in the future many linguists would even argue that English has no future tense

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:52.619
That's because there are no inflections on the main verb to show future tense

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:56.459
But it's shown instead using a modal auxiliary specifically will

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:03.930
modal auxiliaries in general indicate likelihood ability permission and obligation will does express future action

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.479
But syntactically it works like a modal auxiliary

00:07:06.479 --> 00:07:12.479
And there's also the construction going to which can be placed in front of the main verb to indicate the future

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:20.339
Semantically speaking English can express the meaning of the future tense using these constructions, but grammatically they're not really future tense

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:23.849
But again most people tend to think of that as the future tense in English

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:29.880
Indeed many of the verb forms that we tend to think of as tenses include elements other than tense

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:36.959
Take French for instance which is usually thought to have 14 tenses 7 simple tenses and 7 compound tenses

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.620
But the 7 compound tenses contain an element of aspect

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:47.820
Shown through the use of auxiliary verbs and the conditional and subjunctive forms show distinctions in mood the conditional form

00:07:47.949 --> 00:07:51.749
Suggests that one action is dependent upon another and the subjunctive form

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.060
indicates desire or probability

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:59.639
These are moods and even though they have separate verb forms and are often referred to as separate tenses

00:07:59.639 --> 00:08:06.899
They really express both tense and mood so verb tenses may work differently in different languages and some languages may lack

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:08.610
grammatical verb tense entirely

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:13.560
But that doesn't mean that they can't express those different time periods in other ways whether from

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:19.469
Context from the use of time expressions like tomorrow from the use of aspect or from the use of modal auxiliaries

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.829
When some people hear that there are languages with no verb tense

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:25.470
They imagine that the speakers of these languages have no

00:08:25.659 --> 00:08:30.689
Understanding of time that they can't plan for the future or that they can't remember the past or something like that

00:08:30.699 --> 00:08:32.680
But that's not the case at all

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:39.659
We're all humans after all and we all find ways to communicate the same basic things the question of the day does your language have

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:42.419
special verb forms to express past present and future

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:45.598
Does your language contain a large number of tenses that?

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.359
incorporate aspect and mood if you're not quite sure

00:08:48.459 --> 00:08:53.578
Maybe look it up and find out a little bit about it and tell us in the comments down below be sure to follow

